<p>Fonts and icons are copied in by <code>scripts/vendor.mjs</code> from the official packages, so each file's origin can be shown. Brand marks stay trademarks of their owners: use them only to link to that service.</p>

<h2>What is deliberately <em>not</em> here</h2>
<p>No file, stylesheet, script, template, class name, variable name or documentation text from any commercial theme. The build does not read any theme folder. A licence that lets you <em>use</em> a paid theme on a site almost never lets you redistribute it or a modified version of it, so none of one can be in a product.</p>

<h2>Ideas versus expression</h2>
<p>Broadly, copyright protects how something is <em>expressed</em> — the actual code, markup, text, artwork — not the idea behind it. A top bar with a collapsible side nav, pill-shaped buttons, a neutral palette, a popular open-source typeface: these are common conventions that many products share. What would be a problem is reproducing another product's <em>code</em>, or imitating a distinctive overall look so closely that buyers could take one product for the other. Two habits follow:</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Take ideas, write the code.</strong> Decide what a component should do, then write it from a blank file against this system's tokens. Never with another theme's source open beside it.</li>
	<li><strong>Make the product its own thing.</strong> Its own name, its own accent and details, its own demo content and screenshots. Similar in sensibility is fine; interchangeable is not.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Working rules</h2>
<ol>
	<li>Nothing is copied into this repo from a commercial theme — not a snippet, not a class name list, not a settings schema.</li>
	<li>Anything from outside is openly licensed (MIT, ISC, BSD, Apache-2.0, OFL, CC0), arrives through <code>scripts/vendor.mjs</code> or <code>package.json</code>, and gets a row in the table above.</li>
	<li>Keep licence files with what they cover (<code>assets/fonts/OFL.txt</code>, <code>partials/icons/README.md</code>).</li>
	<li>Commit history is evidence of independent work. Commit as you go, with messages that say what was built.</li>
	<li>Do not use another product's name — in the theme's name, its marketing, or its docs.</li>
</ol>
